    "result_text": "Owners can now have an optional `postcode`, and the full test suite passes: 14 tests (the 3 existing acceptance tests, 4 existing util tests and 7 new ones).\n\n**How it behaves**\n- **No postcode:** the owner is accepted as before, so existing requests keep working.\n- **Wrong format** (not exactly 4 digits, e.g. `\"12\"`): rejected with 400. The OpenAPI spec now declares the field with a 4-digit pattern, and Spring's standard request validation enforces it.\n- **Wrong region:** if the city is Sydney, Melbourne or Brisbane, the postcode must fall in 2000\u20132099, 3000\u20133099 or 4000\u20134099 respectively, otherwise the response is 400. The error lists `postcode` as the bad field, in the same format as the existing telephone error. The city-to-region table is the one already used for `locality` (`OwnerMapper.CITY_REGIONS`), so the two can't drift apart.\n- **Other cities:** any 4-digit postcode is accepted.\n- **Storage:** the postcode is saved and returned on `GET`. Updating an owner (`PUT`) runs the same check and saves the new postcode.\n\n**What changed**\n- `openapi.yml`: added `postcode` to the owner fields.\n- `Owner.java`: new `postcode` column.\n- All four database schemas (H2, HSQLDB, MySQL, Postgres): new `postcode VARCHAR(4)` column. The seed data names its columns, so it didn't need changes.\n- `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl`: added `postcode` to the SELECT and UPDATE statements so the JDBC setup also stores it.\n- New `OwnerPostcode` checker and `InvalidPostcodeException`, plus a 400 handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1`: runs the check on create and update.\n\n**New tests**\n- `OwnerPostcodeTests` (5 tests) covers the range edges for each region, unknown cities, a missing postcode and badly formatted ones.\n- `PostcodeRegionAcceptanceTest` (2 tests) covers a valid Melbourne postcode being stored and a Sydney/3000 request getting a 400.\n\nI didn't edit the existing `AcceptanceTest`, because its base class says it belongs to someone else.\n\nOn a PUT, leaving out the postcode clears it, the same way `email` is cleared.",
